    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate leaning heavily on high-upside matchups against Andrew Abbott and Walker Buehler. **Corbin Carroll** is our clear anchor today with an elite 85.10 score; facing a 4.46 xERA arm in Buehler, he benefits from a rare alignment of favorable park factors and the premium #2 lineup slot, making him our primary engine for SB and R production. Meanwhile, the mid-range value is bolstered by **Tyler Soderstrom** (69.51) and **Max Muncy** (67.55). Both benefit from the favorable matchup against Abbott, whose susceptibility to power allows **Max Muncy** to capitalize on his platoon advantage, while **Tyler Soderstrom** remains a locked-in start despite a minor drag from his handedness, as the SP weakness overrides that concern.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical tightropes regarding lineup order and platoon sensitivity. **Brice Turang** (51.43) remains in the lineup primarily for his leadoff speed despite a difficult encounter with Braxton Ashcraft, as we prioritize SB volume above all else in our Roto strategy. On the bench, **Zach McKinstry** (33.54) was the closest contender for a starting spot; however, his sub-40 efficiency score was suppressed by a bottom-of-the-order projection and a daunting matchup against the talented Bryce Miller. Consequently, the optimizer opted for the higher floor offered by **Travis Bazzana**, though we will be monitoring the status of **James Wood** and **Vinnie Pasquantino** closely as they remain sidelined on the IL.\n\nA word of caution: with most of our roster currently carrying \"Lineup Pending\" designations, our projected efficiency scores are built on historical spot assumptions. While **Gabriel Moreno** and **Austin Riley** are currently projected to start, any significant shift in their batting order\u2014specifically moving **Austin Riley** lower than his assumed #8 spot\u2014could necessitate a reassessment of our efficiency thresholds. We are also wary of **Seiya Suzuki**\u2019s start against Dylan Cease; his 47.89 score is barely hanging above the marginal threshold, as the elite skill level of the opposing pitcher makes this a high-risk, low-reward play compared to our other, more favorable matchups against weaker rotations.",
